 "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "500 metres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "12 February 2022 — 16:53"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2140"} "National Speed Skating Oval, Olympic Green, Beijing"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/19020400"} "30 from 15 countries"]]] [:tr {:class "records", :style ""} [:th {:style "vertical-align: top;"} "Olympic Record "] [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/9001025"} "34.41"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/128235"} "Håvard Lorentzen"] " " [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"] " / 19 February 2018 / " [:a {:href "/records/event_name/530"} "Details"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Referee"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/1805307"} "Hanjo Heideman"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Starter"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/1805308"} "Roland Steenbeck"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "China was hoping to get their first male speed skating gold through " [:a {:href "/athletes/136976"} "Gao Tingyu"] ". The 2018 bronze medalist had clocked three sub-34-second races in the season, and set an unofficial world record at the Tomaszów-Mazowiecki World Cup, opening in 9.32 seconds. Gao, however, had plenty of contenders to fight off. Reigning World Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/136925"} "Laurent Dubreuil"] " was also leading the World Cup standings, and two of Japan’s entries – " [:a {:href "/athletes/148562"} "Wataru Morishige"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/148564"} "Tatsuya Shinhama"] " – had also won races earlier in the season. Missing from the field was world record holder " [:a {:href "/athletes/148864"} "Pavel Kulizhnikov"] ". The three-time World Champion had had a poor season and did not qualify for the strong ROC team in this event."] "\n\n" [:p {} "As he had not raced in all World Cup races, Gao was the first of the favorites to start, and he immediately set a tough target for the others. Following a 9.42 opening, he bested the Olympic Record with 34.32. In the tenth pair, 2018 silver medalist " [:a {:href "/athletes/137635"} "Cha Min-Gyu"] " recorded the best full lap of the field (24.75), which landed him at 34.39, second behind. After him, many the outsiders for a medal fell short – Michalski (35.52), Arefyev (34.56), Murakami (34.57). Only in the penultimate pair did Morishige come close to the leading times, 34.49, guaranteeing Gao a medal. The final race between Dubreuil and Shinhama then saw the Canadian controversially false start. Replays showed no signs of a false start, while earlier Gao had not been completely still. Possibly affected, Shinhama then stumbled in the first metres, throwing away his shot at a medal. Dubreuil did continue at full pace, but with 34.52 just fell out of the podium."] "\n\n" [:p {} "With Gao’s gold, Cha’s silver and Morishige’s bronze medal, all the medalists were from Asia, the second time this had occurred, the first time being in Vancouver 2010."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button964900", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button964900').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "100 m"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {: